EXT. THE MARKET ON MOSSBACK'S SHELL - NIGHT

A giant tortoise the size of a hill wades through a silver-grass plain under three moons. Lanterns hang from its shell ridges like fruit. Stalls of woven reeds, hanging spices, strings of glowing beetles. The air smells of smoke and honey.

IXCHEL VANTH, thirties, a spice merchant in a feathered shawl, weighs saffron on a brass scale. BRAM OSSERY, twenties, a young knight with a scuffed breastplate, leans on her counter pretending to browse. THISBE, ageless, a street-musician, plays a hollow-bone flute under a lantern. ULAANA KHAN, forties, in a fur-lined cloak, inspects a jar of black pepper held to the light.

                    IXCHEL
          Three silver for the saffron.

                    BRAM
          Two.

                    IXCHEL
          Three, and you pay for the staring.

Bram drops his eyes to the scale. Ulaana lowers the jar and watches him across the stall. Thisbe's flute dips into a minor key.

                    ULAANA
          You're far from the capital, sir knight.

                    BRAM
          I'm on holiday.

                    ULAANA
          Wearing the king's crest.

He shifts. His cloak falls over the crest. Ixchel slides the scale an inch to the left, away from his hand.

                    IXCHEL
          (low)
          Ulaana. He's a boy.

                    ULAANA
          He's a boy with a king's seal in his pocket.

Bram's fingers move toward his belt. Thisbe stops playing. For a moment the market's murmur is the only sound.

A deep note rolls beneath their feet, the long slow call of the tortoise itself.

                    MOSSBACK (V.O.)
          (vast, unhurried)
          I feel four hearts quicken on my back. Who has a secret?

Everyone stills. Lanterns sway. Thisbe lowers the flute and bows to the ground.

                    THISBE
          Great One. Nobody speaks.

                    MOSSBACK (V.O.)
          Then I will walk slower.

The ground shudders and settles. Ixchel moves first, slowly, and sets the scale on the counter.

                    IXCHEL
          Two silver and a half.

                    BRAM
          That's robbery.

                    IXCHEL
          That's the price of silence.

He pays. She wraps the saffron in a leaf, holds it out. He takes it without meeting her eyes. Ulaana sets down the pepper jar and turns away, her cloak flaring, and walks into the crowd. Thisbe lifts the flute and begins a new tune, bright and quick, as if nothing had happened.
